var e={name:`Firebox`,purpose:`The furnace of the locomotive: a copper box, wrapped in boiler water on every side and above, where coal burns at well over 1,000 °C. Here the chemical energy of the fuel first becomes heat.`,how:`Coal burns on the grate at the bottom while the draught pulls air up through it. The walls and roof of the inner firebox sit in the direct glare of the fire and pass its radiant heat straight into the water behind them. Although the firebox is only a small part of the boiler's heating surface, it often raises a third or more of the steam, because radiation from a white-hot fire moves heat far faster than gas brushing along a tube. The burnt gases then curl back round the brick arch and leave forward through the tubes and flues.`,poweredBy:`Burning coal, about 29 MJ of heat in every kilogram, with air supplied by the smokebox draught.`,connectionNote:`It stands over the grate, is tied to the outer shell by the stays, is cooled by the boiler water around it, and sends its gases round the brick arch into the fire tubes and flues.`,failure:`The firebox survives only because water covers it. If its roof, the crown sheet, is ever left dry, the metal overheats within minutes, softens and can give way under boiler pressure, which is why the water level is watched constantly.`,spec:`Round-top wide firebox over a grate of about 4.3 m²`},t={name:`Grate`,purpose:`The floor of the fire: a bed of cast-iron bars that carries the burning coal and lets air up through it.`,how:`Narrow gaps between the firebars admit 'primary' air from the ashpan below; it feeds the fire and at the same time cools the bars so that they do not melt. Ash falls through the same gaps. The fireman keeps the fire thin, level and bright over the whole area, because a hole in the fire lets cold air rush through, while a thick heap starves itself of air. A grate this large cannot fit between the driving wheels, so it sits behind them, above the trailing truck.`,poweredBy:`Air drawn up through the bars by the partial vacuum that the blastpipe creates in the smokebox.`,connectionNote:`It forms the bottom of the firebox, breathes through the ashpan beneath it, is fed by hand from the coal bunker, and its weight is carried by the trailing truck.`,failure:`When ash and clinker block the air gaps, the fire dulls and steam pressure falls. Bars that lose their cooling air, for instance over a choked ashpan, overheat, sag and can burn through.`,spec:`About 4.3 m² of grate area`},n={name:`Ashpan & Dampers`,purpose:`A steel hopper under the grate that catches ash and hot embers, with hinged damper doors that control how much air reaches the fire from below.`,how:`Everything that falls through the firebars lands here instead of on the track. The dampers are flaps at the front and back of the pan, worked by levers in the cab: opened wide, they let the draught pull plenty of primary air up through the fire; closed down, they throttle it. They are the fireman's coarse control over how hard the fire burns, used together with the firehole door, which supplies air above the fire.`,poweredBy:`The dampers are set by hand from the cab; the smokebox draught does the pulling.`,connectionNote:`It hangs beneath the grate and firebox, shares the job of air control with the firehole door, and its damper levers come up into the cab.`,failure:`A pan left full of ash chokes the air supply and bakes the firebars from below. A damper stuck shut makes the engine steam poorly; one stuck open makes the fire hard to check, so steam is wasted through the safety valves.`},r={name:`Brick Arch`,purpose:`A sloping arch of firebrick inside the firebox that makes the flames take the long way round before they can enter the tubes, so that the fuel burns completely.`,how:`The arch springs from just below the tubes and slopes up and back over the fire. Gases rising from the coal cannot go straight to the tube plate: they must sweep back towards the firehole, mix with the fresh air entering there, and only then turn forward over the top of the arch. That longer, hotter path gives the gas driven out of the coal time to burn instead of escaping as smoke. The glowing bricks also keep the flame hot and shield the tube plate from cold air when the door is opened.`,poweredBy:`The fire itself heats it; the arch has no moving parts and simply steers the gases.`,connectionNote:`It spans the firebox just below the firebox tube plate, works with the air from the firehole door, and shields the ends of the fire tubes behind it.`,failure:`If bricks fall out, the gases short-circuit straight into the tubes: more smoke, less heat from each shovelful, and tube ends exposed to chilling draughts that make them leak.`},i={name:`Firehole Door`,purpose:`The opening in the back of the firebox through which the fireman shovels coal, with a door that also lets a measured amount of air in above the fire.`,how:`When coal is heated, part of it is driven off as gas, and that gas needs 'secondary' air above the fire bed if it is to burn. The door supplies it, through vents or by being left slightly open, and the brick arch makes air and gas mix. Between rounds of firing the door is kept closed, so that a flood of cold air does not chill the firebox and the tube plate.`,poweredBy:`Opened and closed by the fireman; the draught draws the air in.`,connectionNote:`It pierces the back of the firebox inside the cab, faces the coal bunker on the tender, and directs its air towards the brick arch.`,failure:`Left open too long, it chills the firebox and wastes heat; kept tight shut over fresh coal, it causes black smoke. If the draught fails while the door is open, flames can blow back into the cab, which is the danger the blower exists to prevent.`},a={name:`Firebox Stays`,purpose:`Hundreds of metal rods that tie the flat walls and roof of the inner firebox to the outer shell, so that 16 bar of pressure cannot push the plates apart.`,how:`A round barrel holds pressure by its shape, but the firebox walls are nearly flat and would bulge at once. So the inner and outer plates are pinned together at close intervals by stay bolts, screwed through both sheets and riveted over, while longer crown stays hang the firebox roof from the shell above. Each stay carries the pressure acting on its own small patch of plate: about 16 kN, or 1.6 tonnes, for every 10 cm × 10 cm. The stays must also bend a little, because the hot inner box expands more than the cooler outer one.`,poweredBy:`No power source: boiler pressure itself keeps every stay in tension.`,connectionNote:`They cross the water space between the inner firebox and the outer shell that continues the boiler barrel, and the crown stays hold up the sheet that carries the fusible plug.`,failure:`Constant flexing can crack a stay, and a broken stay passes its load to its neighbours. Boiler inspectors therefore test them regularly, because a group of failed stays would let the plate bulge.`,spec:`16 bar on a 10 cm × 10 cm patch ≈ 16 kN (1.6 tonnes) per stay`},o={name:`Fusible Plug`,purpose:`A last-resort alarm in the roof of the firebox: a threaded bronze plug with a core of soft, low-melting metal that warns the crew if the water level falls dangerously low.`,how:`Normally the plug is covered by boiler water at about 204 °C, which keeps its core below melting point even with the fire directly beneath it. If the water level drops far enough to bare the crown, the metal there quickly becomes much hotter, the core melts, and a jet of steam and water roars down into the firebox. The jet is too small to put the fire out or to empty the boiler: its job is to tell the crew to deaden or drop the fire at once.`,poweredBy:`Heat alone: it acts when the crown sheet above the fire is no longer cooled by water.`,connectionNote:`It is screwed into the firebox crown beneath the boiler water, and backs up the water gauge glasses and injectors that normally keep that crown covered.`,failure:`A plug caked in scale, or one whose core has aged, may melt late or not at all, removing the final warning before the crown sheet overheats. Plugs are therefore examined and renewed at fixed intervals.`,spec:`Core alloy melts at roughly 230–330 °C; the water around it is at about 204 °C`},s={name:`Boiler Barrel`,purpose:`The long cylindrical pressure vessel that holds water and steam at 16 bar and contains the fire tubes and flues.`,how:`A cylinder is the natural shape for holding pressure: its steel is simply stretched all round, like the hoops of a barrel, with no flat surface to bulge. At 16 bar every square metre of plate is pushed outward with a force of about 160 tonnes, so the shell is rolled from thick steel plate and its seams are riveted. What you see from outside is not the shell itself but thin sheet-steel cladding over a layer of insulation that keeps the heat in.`,poweredBy:`Nothing drives it; the steam and water inside press on it with 16 bar in every direction.`,connectionNote:`It is closed at the ends by the tube plates, filled with boiler water around the fire tubes and flues, and carries the steam dome on top.`,failure:`Corrosion, scale and fatigue slowly weaken plates and seams. Because the water inside is far hotter than its normal boiling point, a rupture would release its energy violently, so boilers are examined, pressure-tested above their working pressure and re-certified on a strict schedule.`,spec:`16 bar gauge (about 17 bar absolute); 6.8 m between tube plates`},c={name:`Fire Tubes`,purpose:`About 100 slender steel tubes that carry the hot gases from the firebox through the water to the smokebox, packing a very large heating surface into the barrel.`,how:`The gases flow through the inside of each tube; the water is on the outside. Heat passes through the thin tube wall into the water along the whole 6.8 m, so gas that enters at over 1,000 °C leaves at a few hundred degrees, though never cooler than the 204 °C water it is heating. Many small tubes are used instead of one wide flue because together they offer far more wall for the gas to touch.`,poweredBy:`Hot combustion gases, pulled through by the smokebox draught.`,connectionNote:`Each tube is fixed into the two tube plates and runs from the firebox to the smokebox through the boiler water, beneath the larger superheater flues.`,failure:`Tubes choked with soot and ash pass less gas and less heat, so they are swept out regularly. A leaking or burst tube sprays water and steam into the firebox and dulls the fire, and the engine has to be taken out of service for the tube to be replaced.`,spec:`About 100 small tubes, each 6.8 m long; with the 23 flues, roughly 180 m² of heating surface`},l={name:`Superheater Flues`,purpose:`The 23 wide fire tubes in the upper part of the barrel, each large enough to hold a superheater element as well as carry hot gas.`,how:`A flue works like a fire tube, with gas inside and water outside, but it does two jobs at once. Its wall heats the boiler water around it, while the gas streaming through also flows over the superheater element lying inside, which is full of steam. In every flue, then, heat travels outward into water and inward into steam at the same time.`,poweredBy:`Hot combustion gases from the firebox, drawn through by the draught.`,connectionNote:`They run between the tube plates above the fire tubes, from firebox to smokebox, and each one houses a superheater element.`,failure:`Ash and cinders lodging round the elements can block a flue, which lowers the steam temperature and the engine's economy. Like the tubes, flues can also leak where their ends are fixed into the tube plates.`,spec:`23 flues, 6.8 m long, one superheater element in each`},u={name:`Tube Plates`,purpose:`The two thick, drilled end plates that hold every tube and flue: one forms the front wall of the firebox, the other closes the front of the barrel at the smokebox.`,how:`Each plate has about 125 holes. The tube ends are pushed through and expanded, rolled outward from inside, until they grip the plate with a steam-tight joint of metal against metal. In return the tubes act as long stays, tying the two flat plates together against the pressure that tries to force them apart. The firebox tube plate has the hardest life in the boiler: fire on one face, water on the other, and a temperature that swings every time the firehole door opens.`,poweredBy:`Not driven: boiler pressure and the heat of the fire are the loads they bear.`,connectionNote:`They stand 6.8 m apart at the firebox and smokebox ends of the boiler barrel and anchor both ends of every fire tube and flue.`,failure:`Repeated heating and cooling loosens tube ends, which then weep water into the firebox and dull the fire. Cracks can also grow in the narrow bridges of metal between the holes, and the plate must then be repaired.`,spec:`6.8 m apart; about 125 tube holes in each`},d={name:`Boiler Water`,purpose:`The water in the boiler is both the raw material for steam and the coolant that keeps the firebox and tubes from overheating.`,how:`It fills the barrel around the tubes and surrounds the firebox, always standing above the firebox roof. Under 16 bar it cannot boil until it reaches about 204 °C, so the boiler is full of liquid water more than 100 °C hotter than a boiling kettle. Heat from the firebox plates and the tubes turns it into bubbles of steam that rise to the surface. Several tonnes of such hot water also store a great deal of energy, which helps the boiler meet a sudden demand for steam.`,poweredBy:`Heat from the fire, arriving through the firebox plates, fire tubes and flues.`,connectionNote:`It surrounds the firebox, boils into the steam space above, shows its level in the water gauge glasses, is topped up through the clack valves and is purged of sludge by the blowdown valve.`,failure:`Too little water bares the firebox crown, the most dangerous state a boiler can be in. Too much water, or dirty foaming water, is carried over with the steam into the cylinders ('priming'), where it washes away the oil and, being incompressible, can break things.`,spec:`Boils at about 204 °C under 16 bar (100 °C in the open air)`},f={name:`Steam Space`,purpose:`The room above the water line where steam separates from the boiling water and collects, ready for use.`,how:`Bubbles burst at the water surface and the steam gathers above it at full boiler pressure. Because it is still in contact with the water it came from, this 'saturated' steam is at exactly the boiling temperature, about 204 °C, and carries a mist of fine droplets. The space also acts as a small reservoir, letting the engine draw steam for a moment faster than the fire is making it, at the price of a falling pressure.`,poweredBy:`Evaporation from the boiler water beneath it.`,connectionNote:`It lies over the boiler water, feeds the steam dome at its highest point, and is the space to which the safety valves, the pressure gauge and the whistle are connected.`,failure:`If the water level is carried too high, the steam space shrinks and water is more easily swept into the steam pipe. Nothing in the space itself can break, but its pressure is the quantity that every safety device on the boiler exists to limit.`,spec:`Saturated steam at 16 bar and about 204 °C`},p={name:`Safety Valves`,purpose:`Two spring-loaded valves on top of the firebox that open by themselves when the pressure reaches its limit of about 16 bar, so that the boiler can never be over-pressed.`,how:`A strong spring holds each valve disc down on its seat while steam pushes up on it from below. At the set pressure the two forces balance and the valve begins to lift. In a 'pop' valve the escaping steam then acts on a wider lip around the disc, so the valve snaps fully open, discharges a great volume of steam with a roar, and shuts again sharply once the pressure has fallen a little. Between them the two valves can release steam faster than the fire can make it.`,poweredBy:`Steam pressure working against a calibrated spring; no outside power and no human action is needed.`,connectionNote:`They are mounted above the firebox on the steam space of the boiler, and lift at the pressure marked by the red line on the pressure gauge.`,failure:`A valve that sticks shut removes an automatic guard against over-pressure, which is why two are fitted, tested and sealed against tampering. A valve that leaks or will not reseat wastes steam and water and makes pressure hard to keep up.`,spec:`Two pop valves, set to lift at about 16 bar`},m={name:`Blowdown Valve`,purpose:`A valve at the lowest part of the boiler, opened briefly to blast out sludge and over-concentrated water and so keep the boiler water clean.`,how:`Steam leaves the boiler pure; everything that was dissolved in the feedwater stays behind. Over hours of steaming these salts and solids build up, causing foaming at the surface and scale and sludge at the bottom. Opening the blowdown valve for a few seconds lets boiler pressure drive out the dirtiest water, together with the sludge lying in it, and fresh feedwater then dilutes what remains.`,poweredBy:`Boiler pressure itself expels the water as soon as the valve is opened.`,connectionNote:`It drains the lowest water space beside the firebox, and the boiler water it discharges is made good by the injectors while the crew watch the water gauge glasses.`,failure:`If blowing down is neglected, the water foams and primes, and scale insulates the plates so that they run hotter and waste fuel. A valve that will not close again loses water quickly, and the crew must keep the injectors on and reduce the fire.`},h={name:`Steam Dome`,purpose:`A raised chamber on top of the boiler barrel that collects steam at the highest and driest point, as far as possible from the heaving water surface.`,how:`Steam leaving boiling water carries spray with it, and the water surges whenever the engine accelerates, brakes or climbs. By drawing steam from the top of a dome, well above the water line, the engine takes the steam that carries the fewest droplets. The dome also makes a convenient home for the regulator valve, whose inlet stands near the top.`,poweredBy:`Filled from the steam space below at full boiler pressure.`,connectionNote:`It rises from the boiler barrel above the steam space and contains the regulator valve at the mouth of the main steam pipe.`,failure:`The dome itself seldom gives trouble. But if the water level is too high, or the water is foaming, even a dome cannot stop water being carried over towards the cylinders.`},g={name:`Regulator Valve`,purpose:`The engine's throttle: the main valve, inside the steam dome, that admits steam from the boiler to the cylinders or shuts it off.`,how:`When the driver moves the regulator handle, a shaft turns a crank in the dome and lifts the valve off its seat on top of a stand-pipe, letting steam flow down into the main steam pipe. With 16 bar on one side and almost nothing on the other, a large valve would be clamped shut by tonnes of force, so a small pilot valve usually opens first to let the pressures even out before the main valve moves. Opened only a little, the valve throttles the steam and lowers its pressure; opened wide, it passes almost full boiler pressure on towards the steam chests.`,poweredBy:`The driver's hand, through the regulator handle and its shaft.`,connectionNote:`It sits in the steam dome at the entrance to the main steam pipe, takes its steam from the steam space, and is worked by the shaft from the regulator handle in the cab.`,failure:`A regulator that leaks lets steam creep to the cylinders while the engine stands, which is why a standing engine is left in mid-gear with its drain cocks open and its brakes on. One that sticks open is more serious: the driver must then stop the engine with the reverser and the brakes.`},_={name:`Main Steam Pipe`,purpose:`The main steam pipe, which carries steam from the regulator forward through the inside of the boiler to the superheater header in the smokebox.`,how:`The pipe runs through the steam space above the water, so it is surrounded by steam at its own temperature and loses no heat on the way. Whatever passes the regulator flows along it, through the front tube plate, and into the saturated side of the superheater header. It is also called the dry pipe, because it draws its steam from the driest place in the boiler, the top of the dome.`,poweredBy:`The difference in pressure between the boiler and the steam chests whenever the regulator is open.`,connectionNote:`It starts at the regulator valve in the steam dome, passes along the steam space and through the front tube plate, and ends at the superheater header.`,failure:`A crack or a leaking joint inside the boiler lets steam into the pipe even with the regulator shut, so the engine cannot be shut off completely. It then has to be held by the reverser, drain cocks and brakes until it is repaired.`},v={name:`Superheater Header`,purpose:`A hollow casting in the top of the smokebox that shares the steam out among the superheater elements and collects it again once it has been superheated.`,how:`Inside, the header is divided into two separate sets of chambers. Steam from the main steam pipe enters the 'saturated' chambers, and the only way out is through the elements: each one starts at a saturated chamber and returns to a 'superheated' one. From the superheated chambers the steam, now at about 380 °C, leaves by the steam pipes to the cylinders.`,poweredBy:`The flow of steam from the boiler; the header itself has no moving parts.`,connectionNote:`It hangs in the smokebox, receives the main steam pipe, carries both ends of every superheater element, and feeds the steam pipes to the cylinders.`,failure:`A leak at an element joint blows steam into the smokebox, where it is wasted and disturbs the draught on the fire. A crack between the chambers would let saturated steam bypass the elements and lower the steam temperature.`},y={name:`Superheater Elements`,purpose:`Long hairpin loops of steel tube, one in each flue, in which the steam is heated a second time: from about 204 °C to about 380 °C.`,how:`Steam flows inside each element, down the flue towards the firebox and back again, while the hot gases of the fire flow round the outside of it. Away from the water, the steam can at last rise above boiling temperature: it dries out completely and becomes a true gas, at the same pressure but filling nearly half as much volume again. Such steam can lose a good deal of heat to the cylinder walls before any of it condenses back into water, and that is the whole value of superheating. Compared with running on saturated steam, it typically saved a locomotive about a quarter of its coal and a third of its water.`,poweredBy:`Heat from the combustion gases passing through the flues.`,connectionNote:`Each element leaves the superheater header, loops down a flue towards the firebox and returns to the header, which passes the hot steam on to the steam pipes.`,failure:`The elements rely on the steam flowing through them to keep them cool. A burnt or cracked element blows steam into its flue and the smokebox, wasting steam and spoiling the draught, and has to be replaced.`,spec:`Steam raised from about 204 °C to about 380 °C with no rise in pressure`},b={name:`Steam Pipes to Cylinders`,purpose:`Two large pipes, one on each side, that carry the superheated steam from the header in the smokebox down to the steam chests above the cylinders.`,how:`The pipes leave the superheated side of the header, pass out through the smokebox wall and curve down the outside of the engine to the steam chests. They are made wide, with easy bends, because any resistance here lowers the pressure that finally reaches the pistons. Insulation beneath their cladding keeps the steam close to 380 °C.`,poweredBy:`The flow of steam towards the cylinders whenever the regulator is open.`,connectionNote:`They run from the superheater header, out of the smokebox, to the middle of each steam chest, between the two heads of the piston valve.`,failure:`A leaking joint wastes steam and takes power from that cylinder. If the leak is inside the smokebox it also weakens the draught, so the fire and the boiler pressure suffer as well.`},x={name:`Whistle`,purpose:`The locomotive's voice: a steam-blown signal that warns of the train's approach and passes messages to railway staff.`,how:`Pulling the whistle cord opens a small valve, and boiler steam rushes out through a thin ring-shaped slot. The sheet of steam strikes the sharp lip of a hollow bell and flutters in and out across it, setting the column of steam and air inside the bell vibrating, just as breath does across the mouth of a bottle. The length of the bell fixes the note: a long bell sounds deep, a short one shrill.`,poweredBy:`Steam taken directly from the boiler.`,connectionNote:`It takes its steam from the steam space at the top of the boiler and is sounded by a cord or lever in the cab.`,failure:`A whistle that fails does not stop the engine, but a train may not run in ordinary service without an audible warning. A valve that will not close wastes steam until it can be shut off.`},S={name:`Cylinder`,purpose:`The heart of the engine: a smoothly bored cast barrel in which steam pushes a piston to and fro, turning the pressure of the steam into mechanical force.`,how:`The cylinder is double-acting. Steam is admitted to one end, driving the piston along its 660 mm stroke, and then to the other end to drive it back, while the opposite side is open to exhaust, so every stroke is a power stroke. Once the valve has cut off the supply, the steam shut inside goes on pushing as it expands, its pressure falling as it does so. One cylinder is mounted horizontally on each side of the engine, outside the frames.`,poweredBy:`Superheated steam at up to 16 bar and about 380 °C, admitted and released by the piston valve above.`,connectionNote:`It contains the piston, is joined to the steam chest above by the steam ports, has drain cocks at its lowest points, and is bolted to the frames, which take its thrust.`,failure:`A worn or scored bore lets steam blow past the piston and go to waste. Water trapped in a cylinder cannot be compressed and can crack a cylinder cover, which is why drain cocks are fitted.`,spec:`600 mm bore × 660 mm stroke; 0.187 m³ swept per stroke; clearance volume about 8%`},C={name:`Piston`,purpose:`The sliding disc inside the cylinder on which the steam pushes: the place where pressure becomes force.`,how:`High-pressure steam acts on one face while the other is open to exhaust, and the difference drives the piston along the bore. Springy cast-iron rings in grooves round its rim press outward against the cylinder wall, sealing the gap while still letting the piston slide. With 16 bar on its 0.283 m², the piston feels a push of up to 452 kN, about 46 tonnes, and that push changes direction at every stroke.`,poweredBy:`The difference in pressure between live steam on one face and exhaust on the other.`,connectionNote:`It slides in the cylinder, is fixed to the piston rod, receives its steam through the ports at each end, and is oiled by the mechanical lubricator.`,failure:`Worn or broken rings let steam leak across to the exhaust side, costing power and giving an uneven beat at the chimney. A piston working loose on its rod knocks heavily and must be dealt with before it strikes a cylinder cover.`,spec:`600 mm diameter; area 0.283 m²; peak force about 452 kN (46 tonnes-force)`},w={name:`Piston Rod`,purpose:`The polished steel rod that carries the piston's force out of the cylinder to the crosshead.`,how:`One end is fixed to the piston; the other passes out through the rear cylinder cover and is secured in the crosshead. Where it leaves the cylinder it slides through a gland: packing rings that hug the rod closely enough to hold back the steam yet let it move. The rod is pushed and pulled in turn with the whole force on the piston, so it is forged from tough steel and ground smooth. It also takes up about 3% of the piston's rear face, so the two strokes are not quite equal in strength.`,poweredBy:`The piston, which pushes and pulls it with up to 452 kN.`,connectionNote:`It joins the piston to the crosshead through a gland in the rear cover of the cylinder, and is kept in line by the crosshead running on the slide bars.`,failure:`Worn packing lets steam blow out round the rod, wasting it and clouding the driver's view. A scored or bent rod soon destroys its packing, and a broken one disables that side of the engine.`,spec:`About 100 mm diameter; loaded with up to 452 kN in push and pull`},T={name:`Steam Chest`,purpose:`The cylindrical chamber above each cylinder that houses the piston valve and holds the live steam waiting to enter the cylinder.`,how:`Superheated steam from the steam pipe enters the middle of the chest, between the two heads of the piston valve. This is 'inside admission': the hottest, highest-pressure steam is kept in the centre, and the two outer ends of the chest are connected only to the exhaust. As the valve slides, it opens the passages to the cylinder in turn to the live steam in the middle and to the exhaust spaces at the ends.`,poweredBy:`Steam from the steam pipes, at a pressure a little below the boiler's after its passage through regulator, superheater and pipes.`,connectionNote:`It receives the steam pipe at its centre, contains the piston valve, leads through the ports to the cylinder below, and opens at both ends into the exhaust passages.`,failure:`A worn valve liner lets live steam leak straight through to the exhaust. It is heard as a continuous blow at the chimney and paid for in coal and water.`,spec:`Inside admission; holds a 300 mm piston valve`},E={name:`Piston Valve`,purpose:`A spool-shaped sliding valve that lets steam into each end of the cylinder at the right moment, shuts it off, and later opens the same end to exhaust.`,how:`Two ringed heads on one spindle slide in a liner that is pierced by the cylinder's ports, and live steam fills the space between them. When the valve moves forward, the inner edge of its front head uncovers the front port and admits steam, while the rear head uncovers the rear port to exhaust; moving back does the opposite. In mid-position each head overlaps its port by the 38 mm 'lap', and it is this overlap that lets the steam be cut off early and left to expand. Because the steam presses equally on both heads, the valve is balanced and slides easily even at 16 bar.`,poweredBy:`The Walschaerts valve gear, through the valve spindle.`,connectionNote:`It slides in the steam chest over the ports, is moved by the valve spindle, releases used steam to the exhaust passages, and is oiled by the mechanical lubricator.`,failure:`Worn or broken rings let steam blow past the heads, wasting it and blurring the exhaust beats. A valve wrongly set on its spindle treats the two ends of the cylinder unequally, so the engine sounds off-beat and loses power.`,spec:`300 mm diameter; steam lap 38 mm; lead 6 mm; travel about 170 mm in full gear`},D={name:`Steam Ports`,purpose:`The passages that join each end of the valve liner to the matching end of the cylinder. Each one serves in turn as the way in for live steam and the way out for exhaust.`,how:`A port opens into the valve liner as a ring of slots right round its circumference, almost a metre of edge on a 300 mm valve, so even a few millimetres of valve movement uncovers a generous opening. When the valve head moves one way, the port is open to live steam; when it moves the other way, the same port is open to exhaust. The passages are kept short and direct, because their volume is part of the 'clearance' space that must be filled with steam at every stroke without being swept by the piston.`,poweredBy:`Steam flows through them whenever the valve opens a difference in pressure.`,connectionNote:`They lead from the steam chest, where the piston valve covers and uncovers them, to the two ends of the cylinder on either side of the piston.`,failure:`Ports narrowed by baked-on oil restrict the flow, so at speed the cylinder fills poorly and empties against more back-pressure. The engine becomes sluggish and uses more steam for the same work.`,spec:`Clearance volume about 8% of the swept volume`},O={name:`Cylinder Drain Cocks`,purpose:`Small valves at the lowest points of each cylinder, opened from the cab to blow out water that has condensed from the steam.`,how:`Steam that meets cold cylinder walls turns back into water, and water, unlike steam, cannot be compressed. If the piston trapped a pool of it against the cylinder cover, something would have to give. So the driver starts away with the drain cocks open: the first strokes blow the water out in the familiar white clouds beside the cylinders, and the cocks are closed once the cylinders are hot and the steam stays dry.`,poweredBy:`A control in the cab opens them; the pressure in the cylinder does the rest.`,connectionNote:`They are fitted beneath both ends of the cylinder, below the piston's path, and are opened from the cab.`,failure:`Cocks left shut on a cold engine risk a hydraulic lock, which can crack a cylinder cover or bend a rod. Cocks that stick open merely waste steam and hide the line ahead in vapour.`},k={name:`Crosshead`,purpose:`The sliding block that joins the piston rod to the connecting rod and keeps the piston rod moving in a perfectly straight line.`,how:`The piston rod moves straight back and forth, but the connecting rod swings up and down as it follows the crankpin round. Where the two meet, at the gudgeon pin in the crosshead, the slanting rod produces a force at right angles to the stroke. The crosshead's bronze slippers bear on the slide bars, which take that side force, so none of it reaches the piston rod or its gland.`,poweredBy:`The piston rod, passing on the full force on the piston, up to 452 kN.`,connectionNote:`It holds the end of the piston rod, carries the small end of the connecting rod on its gudgeon pin, runs between the slide bars, and drives the union link of the valve gear from an arm beneath it.`,failure:`Worn slippers let the crosshead knock at each end of the stroke. If its bearing surfaces run dry they overheat and score, and the engine must be stopped before the damage spreads to the piston rod.`},A={name:`Slide Bars`,purpose:`Fixed steel guide bars, above and below the crosshead, that keep it on a straight path and absorb the sideways thrust of the slanting connecting rod.`,how:`Whenever the connecting rod is not in line with the piston rod, part of the piston's push is turned into a force at right angles to the stroke. The slide bars, bolted between the rear cylinder cover and a bracket on the frames, resist that force. With the engine running forwards the thrust is upward on both the outward and the return stroke, so the top bar does most of the work; in reverse it is the bottom bar.`,poweredBy:`They are fixed guides that only react against the side thrust of the crosshead.`,connectionNote:`They are held between the back of the cylinder and a motion bracket on the frames, and guide the crosshead against the thrust of the connecting rod.`,failure:`Bars that run short of oil score and overheat, and the crosshead begins to bind. Worn or loose bars let the crosshead lift and knock, which soon damages the piston rod and its gland.`},j={name:`Connecting Rod`,purpose:`The long rod that carries the piston's push from the crosshead to the crankpin of the middle driving wheel, turning straight-line motion into rotation.`,how:`Its small end swings on the gudgeon pin in the crosshead; its big end turns on the crankpin, 330 mm from the centre of the axle. As the crosshead moves to and fro, the rod pushes and pulls the crankpin round in a circle, so one out-and-back movement of the piston makes one turn of the wheels. The rod is forged with an I-shaped cross-section, which keeps it stiff against buckling under a push of up to 452 kN and yet light, for at full speed it is flung up and down more than five times a second.`,poweredBy:`The piston, by way of the piston rod and crosshead.`,connectionNote:`It links the crosshead to the crankpin of the middle driving wheels, the same pin that carries the coupling rods and the return crank.`,failure:`A big-end bearing that runs short of oil overheats quickly and can melt its white-metal lining, giving a heavy knock. The crew must stop at once, because a seized or broken rod at speed can wreck the machinery on that side.`,spec:`About 3.7 m between centres; drives the middle coupled axle`},M={name:`Crankpin`,purpose:`A stout steel pin pressed into each driving wheel, away from its centre, which gives the rods a lever arm with which to turn the wheel.`,how:`The pin sits 330 mm from the centre of the axle, half the piston stroke, so a push on it produces a turning effort, or torque, about the axle. That leverage is greatest when the crank stands at right angles to the rod, and it falls to nothing at the two 'dead centres', where rod, pin and axle lie in one straight line. The pin of the middle wheel is the busiest: it carries the connecting rod, the coupling rods and, on its outer end, the return crank.`,poweredBy:`The connecting rod on the middle wheel; the coupling rods on the other two.`,connectionNote:`It is pressed into the driving wheel and carries the bearings of the connecting rod and coupling rods; on the middle wheel its outer end also holds the return crank.`,failure:`An overheated bearing can score the pin, and a pin that works loose in its wheel throws the rods out of line. Either means stopping the engine and renewing the pin in the workshop.`,spec:`Crank radius 330 mm; up to about 149 kN·m of torque from one cylinder`},N={name:`Coupling Rods`,purpose:`Rods joining the crankpins of the three driving wheels on each side, so that all six wheels turn together and share the work of pulling.`,how:`The connecting rod drives only the middle axle. The coupling rods pass part of its effort to the axles in front and behind, so that the full 60 tonnes resting on the coupled wheels is used for grip instead of the 20 tonnes on one axle alone. Because every crankpin is at the same radius and the same angle, the rods simply travel round with them, always parallel to the rail. A hinged joint lets each axle rise and fall over uneven track without bending the rods.`,poweredBy:`The middle crankpin, driven by the connecting rod.`,connectionNote:`They run between the crankpins of the three driving wheels on each side, taking their drive from the pin they share with the connecting rod and passing it to the outer driving axles.`,failure:`If the wheels slip violently or a bearing seizes, a coupling rod can bend or break. The wheels it served then no longer help to pull, and a loose rod is a danger, so the engine is stopped and the rods are taken down.`,spec:`Three coupled axles, 2.3 m apart`},P={name:`Driving Wheels`,purpose:`The six large wheels that turn the engine's torque into pull at the rail and carry 60 tonnes of its weight.`,how:`Each wheel is a spoked steel centre with a hard steel tyre shrunk on. The crankpin turns the wheel, and friction between tyre and rail turns that rotation into forward thrust, so the pull can never be greater than the grip will bear. Large wheels suit an express engine: one turn carries it 6.28 m, so even at 120 km/h they make only about 320 turns a minute and the pistons are not overworked. Crescent-shaped weights in the rim balance the heavy rods and crankpins.`,poweredBy:`Torque from the connecting rod and coupling rods, acting on the crankpins.`,connectionNote:`They are pressed onto the driving axles, carry the crankpins for the rods, are gripped by the brake blocks, and run on rails that the sanding gear can dust with sand.`,failure:`If the pull exceeds the grip, the wheels spin without moving the train, wearing tyres and rail until the driver eases the regulator. A wheel locked by the brakes slides instead and grinds a flat on its tyre, which then hammers at every turn.`,spec:`2.0 m diameter; 6.28 m per turn; 20 t on each axle`},F={name:`Driving Axles & Axleboxes`,purpose:`The three heavy steel axles that carry the driving wheels, together with their axleboxes: the bearings in which they turn and through which the engine's weight reaches them.`,how:`A wheel is pressed onto each end of the axle with its crank set exactly 90° from the crank on the other side. This 'quartering' ensures that whenever one cylinder is at a dead centre and cannot turn the wheels, the other is in mid-stroke and can. Each axle turns in two oil-fed axleboxes, which can slide up and down in guides in the frames with the springs resting on them. The wheels can therefore follow the track while the frames ride steadily above.`,poweredBy:`Torque arriving through the crankpins and the wheels pressed onto each axle.`,connectionNote:`Each axle carries a pair of driving wheels with their crankpins, and its axleboxes slide in guides in the frames beneath the springs.`,failure:`An axlebox short of oil runs hot, a 'hot box', and can melt its bearing metal, so the engine has to come off its train. Axles are also tested regularly for fatigue cracks, since they are bent first one way and then the other at every turn.`,spec:`Three axles carrying 20 t each; cranks quartered at 90°, right-hand side leading`},I={name:`Leading Bogie`,purpose:`A four-wheeled swivelling truck under the front of the engine that carries the weight of the cylinders and smokebox and steers the locomotive into curves.`,how:`The bogie pivots beneath the front of the frames and can also shift a little sideways against centring springs. On entering a curve its wheels meet the bend first, and the springs nudge the front of the engine round, instead of leaving the big, rigidly held driving wheels to strike the outer rail. This gentle guidance is what lets a long engine run steadily at 120 km/h and more.`,poweredBy:`Unpowered: its wheels roll freely, carrying weight and steering.`,connectionNote:`It pivots beneath the frames close to the cylinders, is sprung like the other axles, and leads the driving wheels into every curve.`,failure:`Weak centring springs or worn pivots let the front end swing from side to side at speed, giving a rough ride and heavy wear on the wheel flanges. A bogie axlebox that runs hot means stopping, as with any other bearing.`,spec:`Four wheels: the '4' in 4-6-2`},L={name:`Trailing Truck`,purpose:`A single carrying axle at the rear that supports the wide firebox behind the driving wheels.`,how:`A powerful boiler needs a large grate, too wide to fit between driving wheels 2 m tall. Placing the firebox behind them, over an axle with small wheels, solves the problem: the axle carries the firebox's weight and can move sideways to let the engine take curves. It is the '2' in the 4-6-2 wheel arrangement, and the feature that made the high-powered Pacific type possible.`,poweredBy:`Unpowered: it carries weight but takes no part in driving.`,connectionNote:`It runs beneath the firebox, grate and ashpan at the rear of the frames, and is sprung like the other axles.`,failure:`Sitting close to the ashpan, its bearings work in heat and dust and can run hot if their oil fails. If its sideways movement becomes stiff, the engine rides roughly through curves.`,spec:`One axle: the '2' in 4-6-2`},R={name:`Return Crank`,purpose:`A short arm fixed to the end of the main crankpin that gives the valve gear a small crank of its own, a quarter of a turn out of step with the piston.`,how:`The arm reaches back from the crankpin towards the centre of the axle, so that the pin at its far end travels round a small circle, about 90° away from the main crank. It does the work of an eccentric on the axle, but out in the open where it can be seen and oiled. The quarter-turn difference is essential: the valve has to be on the move, opening a port, at the very moment the piston pauses at the end of its stroke.`,poweredBy:`The main crankpin, which carries it round once for every turn of the wheels.`,connectionNote:`It is clamped to the outer end of the crankpin on the middle driving wheel and drives the eccentric rod, which rocks the expansion link.`,failure:`If the arm slips round on the crankpin, the valve timing on that side is thrown out: the exhaust beats become uneven and power is lost. It is therefore keyed and locked firmly in place.`,spec:`Its pin circles the axle at a radius of about 180 mm, a quarter-turn behind the main crank when running forwards`},z={name:`Eccentric Rod`,purpose:`The rod that carries the return crank's motion to the foot of the expansion link, rocking the link once for every turn of the wheels.`,how:`One end rides round on the return crank's pin; the other is pinned to the tail of the expansion link, below its pivot. As the wheel turns, the rod pushes and pulls that tail and so makes the link rock to and fro. Because the return crank is set a quarter-turn from the main crank, the rocking is 90° out of step with the piston.`,poweredBy:`The return crank on the main crankpin.`,connectionNote:`It joins the return crank, on the main crankpin, to the foot of the expansion link.`,failure:`Worn bearings at its ends add slack that blurs the valve timing. If the rod breaks, the valve gear on that side is disconnected and the engine is worked home on one cylinder.`},B={name:`Expansion Link`,purpose:`A curved, slotted link, rocking on a fixed pivot, from which the valve can take a large movement, a small one, none at all or the opposite one. It is what makes the engine reversible and its cut-off adjustable.`,how:`The eccentric rod rocks the link about a pivot at its middle, and a die block on the radius rod slides in its curved slot. At the pivot the block does not move at all; the farther from the pivot it is set, the more it moves. In the lower half of the slot it gives forward running, and in the upper half it moves the opposite way as the link swings, which reverses the engine. The slot is curved to match the length of the radius rod, so that shifting the block does not itself disturb the valve.`,poweredBy:`The eccentric rod, rocking it once for every turn of the wheels.`,connectionNote:`It pivots in a bracket on the frames, is rocked by the eccentric rod, and holds in its slot the die block of the radius rod, which the reversing shaft raises and lowers.`,failure:`Wear between die block and slot causes lost motion, most noticeable when running with a short cut-off. Starved of oil, the block can seize in the slot and make the reverser very hard to move.`},V={name:`Radius Rod`,purpose:`The rod that takes whatever movement the die block picks up in the expansion link and delivers it to the top of the combination lever.`,how:`Its rear end carries the die block in the slot of the link; its front end is pinned to the combination lever just above the valve spindle. The lifting link from the reversing shaft raises or lowers the rod and so moves the die block along the slot. That position decides how far, and in which direction, the rod moves the valve: about 73 mm each way in full gear, shrinking to nothing in mid-gear. The rod is exactly as long as the radius of the link's curve, which is how it got its name.`,poweredBy:`The rocking expansion link, by way of the die block.`,connectionNote:`It runs from the die block in the expansion link to the top of the combination lever, next to the valve spindle, and hangs from the lifting link of the reversing shaft.`,failure:`If the rod or its lifting link fails, the die block drops to the end of the slot and that side goes into full gear, out of the driver's control. The engine must be stopped and the gear on that side made safe.`},H={name:`Reversing Shaft & Lifting Link`,purpose:`A cross-shaft with arms and lifting links that raises or lowers the radius rods on both sides of the engine together, setting the direction of travel and the cut-off.`,how:`The reach rod from the reverser in the cab turns the shaft through part of a turn. An arm at each end, working through a lifting link, lowers the radius rod for forward gear or raises it for reverse, sliding its die block along the expansion link. Because a single shaft serves both sides, the left and right valve gears are always set alike.`,poweredBy:`The driver, through the screw reverser and its reach rod.`,connectionNote:`It turns in bearings carried from the frames, is moved by the reach rod from the reverser, and its lifting links hold the radius rods whose die blocks ride in the expansion links.`,failure:`Wear in its joints lets the die blocks wander, so the true cut-off differs a little from what the reverser shows. A broken arm or lifting link drops one radius rod into full gear, and the engine has to be stopped.`},U={name:`Combination Lever`,purpose:`An upright lever that gives the valve a small copy of the piston's own movement, so that the port is already open by the 'lead' when each stroke begins.`,how:`The foot of the lever is driven from the crosshead through the union link; near its top are two pins, the radius rod above and the valve spindle just below. The crosshead moves 330 mm either side of centre, and the lever reduces that to about 44 mm at the valve, exactly in step with the piston. This is the 38 mm lap plus the 6 mm lead, so at each dead centre the valve has taken up its overlap and opened the port by 6 mm, whatever the reverser setting. On top of this the lever passes on the movement of the radius rod, a quarter-turn out of step, and so 'combines' the two.`,poweredBy:`The crosshead, through the union link, together with the radius rod.`,connectionNote:`Its foot hangs from the union link on the crosshead, and its head joins the radius rod and the valve spindle.`,failure:`Worn pins give slack that eats directly into the 6 mm of lead and makes the engine less lively. A broken lever leaves the valve uncontrolled, so the gear on that side has to be disconnected.`,spec:`About 330 mm of crosshead movement becomes about 44 mm at the valve (lap 38 mm + lead 6 mm)`},W={name:`Union Link`,purpose:`A short link joining an arm on the crosshead to the foot of the combination lever.`,how:`The crosshead moves in a dead straight line, but the foot of the combination lever swings in an arc. The union link, pinned at both ends, bridges the two movements without straining either. Through it the valve gear is told, at every instant, exactly where the piston is.`,poweredBy:`The crosshead, moving with the piston.`,connectionNote:`It is pinned to a drop arm on the crosshead, at the end of the piston rod, and to the bottom of the combination lever.`,failure:`Wear in its two pins adds slack to the lap-and-lead movement. If the link breaks, the combination lever swings free and the valve on that side loses its timing, so the engine has to be stopped.`},G={name:`Valve Spindle`,purpose:`The rod that carries the two heads of the piston valve and passes the valve gear's combined movement on to them.`,how:`The spindle leaves the steam chest through a gland and is pinned to the combination lever, with a guide to keep it moving in a straight line. It receives two movements a quarter-turn out of step: about 44 mm each way from the crosshead and up to about 73 mm each way from the radius rod. They add like the two short sides of a right-angled triangle, giving about 85 mm each way, the 170 mm travel of full gear, and only 88 mm in mid-gear. With inside admission the gland has to hold back nothing stronger than exhaust steam.`,poweredBy:`The combination lever, which merges the movements of the crosshead and the radius rod.`,connectionNote:`It runs from the combination lever, beside the radius rod joint, through a gland into the steam chest, where the heads of the piston valve are fixed on it.`,failure:`If the valve heads shift on the spindle, steam is admitted unequally to the two ends of the cylinder and the exhaust beats become uneven. A bent spindle makes the valve bind and overloads the whole valve gear.`,spec:`Valve travel about 170 mm in full gear, about 88 mm in mid-gear`},K={name:`Smokebox`,purpose:`The chamber at the front of the boiler where the gases emerge from the tubes and are swept up the chimney. It is also the vacuum chamber that makes the fire draw.`,how:`The jet of exhaust steam from the blastpipe carries the smokebox gases away up the chimney and leaves a slight partial vacuum behind. As the smokebox is sealed everywhere else, air can get in to fill that vacuum only by way of the ashpan, the fire and the whole length of the tubes and flues: this flow is the draught. The smokebox also houses the superheater header and the steam pipes, and collects the ash and cinders carried through the tubes.`,poweredBy:`The exhaust steam jet from the blastpipe or, when the regulator is shut, the blower.`,connectionNote:`It is closed at the back by the front tube plate, receives gas from the fire tubes and flues, contains the blastpipe and the superheater header, and carries the chimney.`,failure:`Any air leak, such as a badly sealed door or a loose joint, destroys the vacuum, and the engine will not steam however well it is fired. Ash left to pile up can block the lower tubes and may burn against the door.`},q={name:`Blastpipe`,purpose:`A nozzle in the smokebox that fires the exhaust steam from the cylinders up the chimney, using it as a jet pump to draw air through the fire.`,how:`The exhaust from both cylinders is brought together and released through a single opening exactly beneath the chimney. The fast jet drags the surrounding smokebox gases along with it, and fresh air is pulled through the fire to replace them. The arrangement regulates itself: the harder the engine works, the more steam it exhausts, the stronger the draught and the fiercer the fire, which makes more steam. The size of the opening is a compromise, for a smaller nozzle gives a keener draught but more back-pressure against the pistons.`,poweredBy:`Exhaust steam leaving the cylinders: four puffs for every turn of the wheels.`,connectionNote:`It stands in the smokebox on top of the exhaust passages from both cylinders, aimed up the chimney, with the blower ring at its tip.`,failure:`A nozzle out of line with the chimney, or furred up with carbon, spoils the draught, and the boiler no longer steams freely. One that is too small makes plenty of steam but chokes the cylinders and costs power at speed.`,spec:`Single blastpipe; four exhaust beats per turn of the wheels`},J={name:`Chimney & Petticoat`,purpose:`The outlet for exhaust steam and smoke. With the petticoat, its flared extension reaching down into the smokebox, it forms the mixing tube of the engine's jet pump.`,how:`The steam jet from the blastpipe spreads as it rises, and the petticoat gathers it in together with the smokebox gases it has caught up. Inside the chimney the jet fills the bore from wall to wall like a moving piston, so that nothing can slip back down past it, and the mixture is thrown clear of the engine. The proportions and alignment of blastpipe, petticoat and chimney matter far more than the chimney's height.`,poweredBy:`The momentum of the exhaust steam jet.`,connectionNote:`It stands on the smokebox directly above the blastpipe and blower, and discharges the steam brought by the exhaust passages together with the gases of the fire.`,failure:`If the jet does not fill the chimney, because of misalignment or a damaged petticoat, air is drawn back down beside it and the draught collapses. The engine then steams badly although nothing looks wrong from outside.`},Y={name:`Exhaust Passages`,purpose:`Channels cast into the cylinder castings and the saddle between them, which lead the used steam from the ends of the steam chests to the base of the blastpipe.`,how:`When the piston valve opens a port to exhaust, the expanded steam leaves through the outer end of the steam chest and enters these passages. Those from the left and right cylinders meet beneath the blastpipe. They are made as wide and as smoothly curved as the casting allows, because any resistance here becomes back-pressure on the pistons, which have to push the old steam out.`,poweredBy:`The pressure still left in the used steam, helped by the returning piston that sweeps it out.`,connectionNote:`They run inside the cylinder casting from the two ends of each steam chest, beyond the heads of the piston valve, to the foot of the blastpipe.`,failure:`Deposits of burnt oil slowly narrow the passages and raise the back-pressure, so the engine feels choked at speed. A crack in the casting lets exhaust escape, weakening the blast and with it the draught.`},X={name:`Blower`,purpose:`A ring of small steam jets at the top of the blastpipe that keeps a draught on the fire whenever the engine itself is not exhausting.`,how:`With the regulator shut there is no exhaust, and the natural draught stops. Opening the blower valve in the cab sends live steam from the boiler to a ring of fine holes round the blastpipe, and these jets pull the gases up the chimney just as the exhaust does, only more gently. The crew turn it on before shutting the regulator, before entering a tunnel and while standing, so that flames and smoke always go forward through the tubes and never back into the cab.`,poweredBy:`Live steam from the boiler, through a valve in the cab.`,connectionNote:`Its ring sits on the blastpipe beneath the chimney in the smokebox, its valve is in the cab, and its purpose is to keep the gases of the firebox flowing forward.`,failure:`Without a working blower, shutting the regulator can let flames blow back through the firehole into the cab. Raising steam from cold also becomes very slow, since a standing engine has no other draught.`},Z={name:`Injector`,purpose:`A pump with no moving parts that uses a jet of steam to force feedwater into the boiler against the boiler's own pressure. Two are fitted, so that one is always in reserve.`,how:`Boiler steam expands through a nozzle to very high speed and meets cold water from the tender in a tapering cone, where it condenses and hands its momentum to the water. The result is a dense, fast jet of warm water, which is then slowed in a widening cone so that its speed turns back into pressure: more than the boiler's own, enough to lift the clack valve. The trick works because water is so dense that pushing a litre of it into the boiler takes less than 2 kJ, a tiny fraction of the energy in the steam jet. Nearly all the steam's heat returns to the boiler with the feedwater, so very little is wasted.`,poweredBy:`Live steam from the boiler, turned on by the fireman.`,connectionNote:`It hangs below the cab, draws cold water from the tender tank, and delivers it along the feedwater pipes to a clack valve and so into the boiler water.`,failure:`An injector will not work if the feedwater is too warm to condense the steam, or if its cones are worn, scaled or blocked: it then spills water from its overflow instead of feeding. If both injectors failed, the crew would have to throw out the fire before the water level fell too far.`,spec:`Two live-steam injectors, each able to feed against 16 bar`},Q={name:`Clack Valve`,purpose:`A non-return valve at the point where feedwater enters the boiler: it lets water in and stops boiler water and steam from coming back out.`,how:`The valve rests on its seat, held shut by boiler pressure. When an injector is working, its delivery pressure is slightly higher than the boiler's, so the valve lifts and water flows in; the moment the injector stops, boiler pressure claps the valve shut again with the 'clack' that gives it its name. The valves are placed on the barrel, well away from the firebox, so that the cooler feedwater mixes into the bulk of the boiler water before it meets the hottest plates.`,poweredBy:`The feedwater itself: delivery pressure from the injector opens it, boiler pressure closes it.`,connectionNote:`It is mounted on the boiler barrel at the end of the feedwater pipe from an injector, and opens into the boiler water.`,failure:`Grit or scale on the seat can hold the valve open, so that boiler water blows back down the feed pipe and that injector cannot be used. A valve stuck shut blocks the feed altogether, leaving the other injector to supply the boiler alone.`},$={name:`Feedwater Pipes`,purpose:`The pipes that bring cold water from the tender to the injectors and carry the injectors' delivery forward to the clack valves on the boiler.`,how:`Flexible hoses bridge the gap between tender and engine, so that the pipes can follow the movement between the two vehicles. Water runs down by gravity from the tank to the injectors under the cab. From each injector a delivery pipe, now carrying warm water at more than boiler pressure, runs forward along the boiler to its clack valve.`,poweredBy:`Gravity from the tender tank on the supply side; the injector's delivery pressure beyond it.`,connectionNote:`They run from the tender tank, across the gap between tender and engine, to the injectors, and on from the injectors to the clack valves.`,failure:`A leak on the supply side lets the injector suck in air, and it will not pick up its water. In hard frost, water standing in the pipes can freeze and split them, so they are drained or kept warm.`},ee={name:`Water Gauge Glasses`,purpose:`Two glass tubes on the back of the boiler, directly in front of the crew, that show how high the water stands inside.`,how:`Each glass is connected to the boiler at both ends: the top to the steam space, the bottom to the water. With the same pressure at both ends, the water in the glass settles at the same height as the water in the boiler. The bottom of the glass is set safely above the firebox roof, so water in sight means that the crown is covered. The crew prove the reading regularly by blowing the gauge through with its test cocks.`,poweredBy:`Nothing at all: water simply finds its own level.`,connectionNote:`The glasses are mounted in the cab on the back of the firebox, joined to the boiler water below and the steam space above, and they tell the fireman when to work the injectors.`,failure:`A blocked passage makes a gauge show a false level, the most dangerous fault it can have, which is why two separate gauges are fitted and tested. If a glass breaks, its cocks are shut and the engine carries on with the other.`,spec:`Two independent gauges; the bottom of each glass is above the firebox crown`},te={name:`Tender Water Tank`,purpose:`The water tank that forms most of the tender's body, holding the 34,000 litres that the boiler will turn into steam.`,how:`The tank lies beneath and around the coal space and is filled through a hatch on top from a water column at the lineside. Baffle plates inside stop 34 tonnes of water from surging when the train brakes or accelerates. At the front, strainers and shut-off valves lead to the feed hoses for the two injectors. A working locomotive uses several times more water than coal by weight, so it is the tank, not the coal bunker, that decides how far it can run without stopping.`,poweredBy:`Gravity: the water simply runs down to the injectors.`,connectionNote:`It is built into the tender beneath the coal bunker and supplies the injectors through the feedwater pipes.`,failure:`If the tank runs dry, the injectors have nothing to feed and the fire must be dropped before the boiler level falls. A blocked strainer has the same effect on one injector at a time.`,spec:`About 34,000 litres (34 tonnes) of water`},ne={name:`Air Compressor`,purpose:`A steam-driven pump on the right-hand side of the engine that supplies the compressed air for the brakes.`,how:`Steam drives a piston to and fro in a steam cylinder, and its rod works the air pistons directly, with no crank or flywheel; a small valve reverses the steam at the end of every stroke. The pump is compound: it squeezes the air in two stages, first in a large low-pressure cylinder and then in a smaller high-pressure one, which takes less work than doing it in a single step. A governor shuts off the steam when the main reservoir is full and starts the pump again when the pressure falls, giving the slow panting heard from an engine at rest.`,poweredBy:`Steam from the boiler, controlled by a pressure governor.`,connectionNote:`It takes steam from the boiler's steam space and pumps air into the main reservoir, which supplies the driver's brake valve and, through it, the brake pipe.`,failure:`If the compressor stops, the air already stored will work the brakes for a while, but it cannot be replaced. As the pressure leaks away the brakes come on by themselves, so the train stops safely but cannot go on.`,spec:`Steam-driven, two-stage (compound) pump`},re={name:`Main Air Reservoir`,purpose:`A steel tank slung under the running boards that stores the compressed air made by the compressor until the brakes need it.`,how:`The compressor fills the reservoir to a pressure well above the 5 bar of the brake pipe, typically 8 to 10 bar. That reserve lets the driver recharge the brake pipe of the whole train quickly after a brake application, far faster than the pump could manage alone. Air warmed by compression cools here, and the water that condenses out of it is drained off, so that it cannot reach the brake valves and freeze.`,poweredBy:`Compressed air delivered by the air compressor.`,connectionNote:`It hangs beneath the running boards, is filled by the air compressor, and feeds the driver's brake valve, which charges the brake pipe.`,failure:`A leaking reservoir or pipe joint keeps the compressor running continuously. If its pressure cannot be kept up, the brakes cannot be released and the train stays where it is.`,spec:`Stores air well above the brake pipe's 5 bar, typically at 8–10 bar`},ie={name:`Brake Cylinder`,purpose:`An air cylinder that turns compressed-air pressure into the mechanical force that presses the brake blocks against the wheels.`,how:`When the brakes are applied, air is let into the cylinder and pushes a piston outward against a return spring. The piston rod pulls on the brake rigging, whose levers multiply the force. When the air is released, the spring returns the piston and the blocks swing clear of the wheels. It is the same principle as the steam cylinder on a smaller scale: force equals pressure times piston area.`,poweredBy:`Compressed air, admitted when the pressure in the brake pipe falls.`,connectionNote:`Its piston pulls the rigging of the brake blocks on the driving wheels, and it is filled in response to the pressure in the brake pipe, which the driver's brake valve controls.`,failure:`A leaking piston seal lets the air escape, so the brake on that vehicle slowly fades during a long application. The brake cylinders on the rest of the train are unaffected, which is one of the strengths of the system.`},ae={name:`Brake Blocks & Rigging`,purpose:`Cast-iron blocks, with the levers and rods that work them, which press against the treads of the wheels to slow the train.`,how:`The rigging is a set of levers and pull-rods that multiplies the force of the brake cylinder and shares it equally among the blocks. Pressed against the turning tyres, the blocks create friction that turns the train's energy of motion into heat. There is a great deal of it: a train of about 350 tonnes at 120 km/h carries nearly 200 MJ, as much heat as burning 7 kg of coal. The blocks must never grip harder than the wheels grip the rail, because a locked, sliding wheel brakes worse than a turning one.`,poweredBy:`The pull of the brake cylinder's piston.`,connectionNote:`The rigging hangs from the frames, is pulled by the brake cylinder, and presses its blocks against the treads of the driving wheels.`,failure:`Blocks wear away steadily and are replaced as routine; if they wear too thin, the piston runs out of travel and the braking weakens. Rigging adjusted too tightly makes the blocks drag, overheating the tyres and wasting power.`,spec:`Cast-iron blocks on all six coupled wheels`},oe={name:`Brake Pipe`,purpose:`The air pipe that runs the whole length of the train, joined by hoses between the vehicles. It both supplies the brakes with air and carries the driver's commands to them.`,how:`In normal running the pipe is kept charged at 5 bar, which holds every brake off and fills a small reservoir on each vehicle. To brake, the driver lets some air out: a control valve on each vehicle senses the fall in pressure and passes air from its own reservoir into its brake cylinder. The bigger the fall, the harder the brakes go on, and restoring 5 bar releases them. Because it is a fall in pressure that applies the brakes, a burst hose or a train breaking in two stops both parts automatically.`,poweredBy:`Compressed air from the main reservoir, fed in through the driver's brake valve.`,connectionNote:`It runs from the driver's brake valve, fed by the main reservoir, past the brake cylinders, across the couplings and through every carriage.`,failure:`A burst hose or an open cock empties the pipe and applies every brake on the train: an inconvenient failure, but a safe one. A cock left closed between vehicles is more serious, because the brakes behind it no longer respond, which is why the brakes are tested before every departure.`,spec:`Charged to 5 bar; the brakes apply when the pressure falls`},se={name:`Driver's Brake Valve`,purpose:`The driver's brake control: a valve in the cab that raises, holds or lowers the pressure in the brake pipe.`,how:`In the running position it connects the main reservoir to the brake pipe through a regulating valve that keeps the pipe at 5 bar. To brake, the driver moves the handle to let a measured amount of air out of the pipe and then holds the pressure steady: a small reduction gives gentle braking, a larger one firmer braking. Swinging the handle right over empties the pipe to the open air for an emergency stop. Returning it to the running position recharges the pipe and releases the brakes.`,poweredBy:`The driver's hand; the air it controls comes from the main reservoir.`,connectionNote:`It stands in the cab at the driver's side, between the main reservoir and the brake pipe, and through the pipe it commands every brake cylinder on the train.`,failure:`A valve that leaks or sticks makes braking hard to judge, and the engine may not work a train until it is put right. Even then the brakes can still be applied by emptying the pipe elsewhere, as a passenger's emergency handle does.`},ce={name:`Cab`,purpose:`The crew's shelter and workplace at the rear of the engine, where driver and fireman have every control and gauge in front of them.`,how:`The back of the firebox forms the front wall of the cab, and on it are grouped the firehole door, the water gauges, the pressure gauge and the valves for the injectors and blower. The driver sits on the right, with the regulator handle, the reverser and the brake valve to hand and a view forward along the boiler. The fireman works across the floor between the coal on the tender and the firehole, and looks after the water. Between them they balance fire, water and steam against the demands of the road.`,poweredBy:`Its crew: one driver and one fireman.`,connectionNote:`It houses the regulator handle, the reverser and the driver's brake valve on the driver's side, with the water gauge glasses and the firehole door on the back of the firebox.`,failure:`There is little in the cab itself to fail, but it is where failures are caught: a misread gauge or a neglected water level begins here. That is why the vital instruments are placed where the crew cannot help seeing them.`},le={name:`Regulator Handle`,purpose:`The driver's throttle lever in the cab, which opens and closes the regulator valve in the steam dome.`,how:`Moving the handle turns a long shaft that runs forward from the cab to the dome, where a crank on its end works the valve. A steam-tight gland seals the point where the shaft passes into the boiler. The handle gives the driver delicate control: a touch of steam to ease a heavy train into motion without slipping, then wide open once the engine is running and the cut-off has been shortened.`,poweredBy:`The driver's arm.`,connectionNote:`It is mounted in the cab within reach of the reverser, and its shaft links it to the regulator valve in the steam dome.`,failure:`A stiff or leaking gland makes the handle heavy and fine control difficult. If the linkage broke with the valve open, the driver would stop the engine by winding the reverser to mid-gear and applying the brakes.`},ue={name:`Reverser`,purpose:`A handwheel and screw on the driver's side that sets the valve gear: forwards or backwards, and how early in each stroke the steam is cut off.`,how:`Turning the wheel moves a nut along a screw, and the nut pushes or pulls the reach rod, which turns the reversing shaft and shifts the die blocks in the expansion links. A scale shows the setting, from full forward gear with a cut-off of about 77%, through mid-gear, where the engine has almost no drive in either direction, to full reverse. The screw allows the fine adjustment needed at speed and holds its setting against the forces that the moving valve gear sends back. Drivers start in full gear and wind the reverser back as speed rises, using less steam at every stroke.`,poweredBy:`The driver's hand on the wheel.`,connectionNote:`It stands in the cab beside the regulator handle, and its reach rod runs forward to the reversing shaft, which sets the die blocks in the expansion links.`,failure:`If the screw or its catch wears, the gear can creep away from its setting and waste steam. A reverser too stiff to wind tempts the driver to leave the cut-off long, which costs coal and water.`,spec:`Cut-off about 77% in full gear, falling to about 7% in mid-gear`},de={name:`Boiler Pressure Gauge`,purpose:`The dial in the cab that shows the steam pressure in the boiler: the fireman's guide to how well the fire is keeping up with demand.`,how:`Inside is a Bourdon tube, a flattened metal tube bent into an arc and closed at one end. Pressure inside the tube makes it try to straighten, and a small linkage and gear magnify that tiny movement to swing the needle. The dial reads 'gauge' pressure, the pressure above that of the surrounding air, and a red line marks the 16 bar at which the safety valves lift. A loop in the connecting pipe stays full of water and keeps live steam away from the delicate tube.`,poweredBy:`Boiler pressure itself, acting inside the Bourdon tube.`,connectionNote:`It is mounted in the cab and piped to the steam space of the boiler, and its red line marks the pressure at which the safety valves lift.`,failure:`A gauge that reads wrongly can mislead the crew, although the safety valves protect the boiler whatever the dial says. Gauges are therefore checked at regular intervals against a calibrated test gauge.`,spec:`Red line at 16 bar gauge, about 17 bar absolute`},fe={name:`Main Frames`,purpose:`The steel backbone of the locomotive: two long frames that carry the boiler, hold the cylinders and axles in line, and pass the pull back to the train.`,how:`The frames run the length of the engine, one on each side, braced apart by cross-stays, the cylinder castings and the buffer beam. Openings in them hold the axleboxes, which slide up and down beneath the springs. Every piston stroke tries to push the cylinder one way and the driving axle the other with up to 452 kN, and it is the frames between them that resist. The boiler is fixed to them only at the smokebox and is free to slide at the firebox end, because it grows a centimetre or two longer as it heats up.`,poweredBy:`They are loaded by the engine's weight and by the alternating thrust of the pistons.`,connectionNote:`They carry the boiler barrel, hold the cylinders and the buffer beam at the front, and rest through the springs on the axleboxes of the driving axles.`,failure:`Years of alternating piston thrust can start fatigue cracks, usually at the corners of the axlebox openings. Loose or cracked frames let axles and cylinders move out of line, so bearings run hot and the engine rides roughly until it is repaired.`,spec:`Carry a locomotive of about 110 t, 60 t of it on the coupled wheels`},pe={name:`Suspension Springs`,purpose:`Steel springs between the frames and every axlebox that carry the engine's weight and absorb the unevenness of the track.`,how:`Each axlebox supports the frames through a leaf spring, a stack of steel plates that bends under load. A wheel can then rise and fall over a poor rail joint without lifting the whole 110-tonne engine with it. Neighbouring springs are often linked by pivoted beams that share the load between axles, so that no wheel is left light in a dip, where it would lose its grip, or overloaded on a hump.`,poweredBy:`The weight of the engine loads them; the unevenness of the track makes them work.`,connectionNote:`They sit between the frames and the axleboxes of the driving axles, with similar springs on the leading bogie and the trailing truck.`,failure:`A broken spring leaf lets the engine settle at that corner and throws weight onto the neighbouring wheels. The engine rides badly and grips unevenly, and is taken out of traffic to have the spring changed.`},me={name:`Buffer Beam & Coupling`,purpose:`The stout beam across the front of the frames, carrying the buffers that take pushing forces and the coupling hook that takes pulling forces.`,how:`Two sprung buffers cushion the contact when the engine meets another vehicle and keep the two a fixed distance apart. Between them is the draw hook with its screw coupling: a link dropped over the other vehicle's hook and tightened with a screw until the buffers just touch. The hoses beside the coupling connect the brake pipe. The beam spreads all these loads into the two main frames.`,poweredBy:`Loaded only by the pushes and pulls between the engine and whatever it is coupled to.`,connectionNote:`It is fixed across the front of the frames near the headlamps, carries the front hose of the brake pipe, and has buffers and a coupling like those behind the tender.`,failure:`A heavy blow in shunting can bend the beam or break a buffer spring, leaving the engine unfit to couple safely. A damaged hook or coupling screw is replaced before the engine may haul or be hauled.`},he={name:`Running Boards`,purpose:`The walkways along both sides of the boiler that let crew and fitters reach the fittings on top and the machinery below.`,how:`Standing on brackets from the frames, the boards give safe footing for oiling round, filling the sandbox and inspecting the boiler fittings. They also serve as a shelf for equipment: the main air reservoirs hang beneath them and pipes run along their edges. Handrails on the boiler complete the route from the cab to the smokebox.`,poweredBy:`A fixed platform, carried on brackets from the frames.`,connectionNote:`They are bracketed from the frames alongside the boiler barrel, with the main air reservoirs beneath them and the sand dome within reach above.`,failure:`Loose plates or brackets are a hazard to anyone walking on them and to the reservoirs and pipes they carry, so they are checked at every routine examination.`},ge={name:`Sand Dome & Sanding Gear`,purpose:`A box of dry sand on top of the boiler, with pipes that lay sand on the rails just ahead of the driving wheels when they need more grip.`,how:`Steel wheels grip steel rails well when both are dry, but poorly when the rail is wet or greasy. The sand is kept on the boiler, where the warmth keeps it dry and free-running. When the driver works the sanding control, sand runs down the pipes, usually helped along by compressed air, and is crushed between wheel and rail. There it bites into both surfaces and can more than double the grip on a slippery rail.`,poweredBy:`Gravity, usually helped by compressed air, released by a control in the cab.`,connectionNote:`It sits on the boiler barrel, its pipes lead down to the rails just in front of the driving wheels, and its control is in the cab.`,failure:`Damp sand cakes and blocks the pipes, usually just when it is wanted on a wet rail. Without sand a heavy train may be unable to start, or may slip to a stand on a gradient.`,spec:`Steel on steel: friction about 0.10–0.15 on a wet or greasy rail, 0.25–0.33 dry or sanded`},_e={name:`Mechanical Lubricator`,purpose:`A box of small oil pumps that feeds measured drops of oil to the pistons and valves, places no oil can could reach while the engine is running.`,how:`A rocking arm driven from the valve gear turns a ratchet, which works a row of tiny plunger pumps. Each pump forces a few drops of thick cylinder oil through a fine pipe to one particular point: a valve liner, a cylinder barrel, a gland. Because it is driven by the motion, it delivers oil in step with the distance run and stops when the engine stops. The oil has to withstand superheated steam at about 380 °C without burning away.`,poweredBy:`A link from the valve gear, so that it pumps only while the wheels are turning.`,connectionNote:`Fine pipes run from it to the steam chest and piston valve and to the cylinder and piston on each side.`,failure:`If a feed stops, the valve or piston it serves begins to run dry: rings and liners score, the valve gear stiffens and wear increases sharply. Crews therefore check the oil level and the working of the pumps before every run.`},ve={name:`Headlamps & Turbo-generator`,purpose:`Electric lamps at the front of the engine, together with the small steam turbine and dynamo that power them.`,how:`A jet of boiler steam spins a small turbine wheel at high speed, and a dynamo on the same shaft generates electricity for the headlamps and the cab lights. A governor keeps the speed, and with it the voltage, steady. The lamps are there less to light the track ahead than to make the train visible to signalmen, track workers and anyone at a level crossing.`,poweredBy:`A small steam turbine fed from the boiler, driving a dynamo.`,connectionNote:`The lamps are carried at the front of the engine, around the buffer beam and smokebox; the turbo-generator takes its steam from the steam space of the boiler and also lights the cab.`,failure:`If the generator fails, the lamps go out and the crew fall back on oil lamps. The engine itself is unaffected, but a train may not run in darkness without lights.`},ye={name:`Tender`,purpose:`The vehicle coupled permanently behind the engine to carry its supplies: 10 tonnes of coal and 34,000 litres of water.`,how:`A locomotive of this size consumes fuel and water far too quickly to carry them on its own frames. The tender is in effect a wagon built round a water tank, with a coal space on top that slopes down towards the cab, where a hinged plate bridges the gap for the fireman. It rides on two four-wheeled bogies, has brakes of its own, and passes the engine's pull on to the train.`,poweredBy:`Hauled by the locomotive through the drawbar.`,connectionNote:`It comprises the coal bunker and the water tank, rides on the tender bogies, and is joined to the cab end of the engine by the drawbar.`,failure:`The tender rarely fails mechanically, but running out of what it carries stops the engine as surely as any breakdown. The water runs out first, so it is water stops, not coal, that set the engine's range.`,spec:`About 34,000 litres of water and 10 t of coal, on two bogies`},be={name:`Coal Bunker`,purpose:`The open space on top of the tender that carries the engine's fuel: about 10 tonnes of coal.`,how:`The floor slopes forward, so that as the fireman digs coal out at the front, the shaking of the tender brings more down towards the shovelling plate. From there every shovelful is swung across the cab floor and through the firehole door. Each kilogram holds about 29 MJ of chemical energy, and an engine working hard burns well over a tonne of coal an hour, all of it moved by hand.`,poweredBy:`Gravity and the fireman's shovel.`,connectionNote:`It sits on the tender above the water tank and faces the firehole door, through which its coal reaches the grate.`,failure:`Poor coal, full of dust or stone, burns badly and forms clinker on the grate, so the engine steams poorly however skilfully it is fired. Coal lying too far back must be dragged forward by hand, which tires the fireman on a long run.`,spec:`About 10 t of coal, with about 29 MJ in every kilogram`},xe={name:`Tender Bogies`,purpose:`Two four-wheeled swivelling trucks that carry the weight of the tender and guide it through curves.`,how:`Each bogie pivots beneath the tender, so that its wheels can follow the rails round a bend while the long tank body stays straight. Springs over the axleboxes cushion a load that changes greatly during a run, as tens of tonnes of coal and water are used up. The tender's wheels are braked as well, adding their share to the stopping power of the train.`,poweredBy:`Unpowered wheels, rolling under the tender's weight.`,connectionNote:`They support the tender with its water tank and coal bunker, and their brakes are worked from the brake pipe.`,failure:`As on any railway vehicle, an axlebox that runs short of oil overheats and has to be attended to. Worn pivots or springs make the tender ride roughly.`},Se={name:`Drawbar & Couplings`,purpose:`The connections that pass the pull down the train: a solid drawbar between engine and tender, and a screw coupling with buffers between the tender and the carriages.`,how:`Engine and tender are never parted in service, so they are joined by a heavy forged bar on pins, with sprung buffers to keep the pair steady. At the back of the tender is a draw hook and screw coupling like the one at the front of the engine, tightened until the buffers of the two vehicles touch, so that the train starts and stops without jerks. The force in that rear coupling is the 'drawbar pull': what remains of the engine's tractive effort after it has moved itself and its tender, and the true measure of its useful work.`,poweredBy:`The tractive effort of the driving wheels, passed back through the frames.`,connectionNote:`The drawbar joins the engine's frames to the tender; the tender's rear coupling and buffers, like those on the buffer beam, connect to the carriages alongside the hoses of the brake pipe.`,failure:`If a coupling breaks, the train divides: the brake hoses pull apart, the air escapes, and the brakes stop both portions automatically. Couplings and drawbars are therefore made with a large margin of strength and examined regularly.`},Ce={name:`Passenger Carriages`,purpose:`The four passenger carriages are the locomotive's load, and the reason for all the machinery ahead of them.`,how:`Each carriage rides on two bogies and is joined to its neighbours by screw couplings and buffers. Steel wheels roll so easily on steel rails that, at low speed on level track, a pull of only a few thousandths of the train's weight keeps it moving, which is how one engine can haul hundreds of tonnes. Every carriage also has brake equipment of its own, a reservoir, a control valve and a brake cylinder, all commanded through the brake pipe.`,poweredBy:`The drawbar pull of the locomotive.`,connectionNote:`They are coupled behind the tender by the drawbar and couplings, and the brake pipe runs through every one of them.`,failure:`A dragging brake or a hot axlebox on a carriage adds resistance and has to be put right before the train continues. If a carriage comes uncoupled, the falling pressure in the brake pipe stops it automatically.`,spec:`Four bogie carriages of roughly 42 t each, with screw couplings and buffers`},we={firebox:e,grate:t,ashpan:n,brickArch:r,fireholeDoor:i,stays:a,fusiblePlug:o,boilerBarrel:s,fireTubes:c,flues:l,tubePlates:u,boilerWater:d,steamSpace:f,safetyValves:p,blowdown:m,steamDome:h,regulatorValve:g,dryPipe:_,superheaterHeader:v,superheaterElements:y,steamPipes:b,whistle:x,cylinder:S,piston:C,pistonRod:w,steamChest:T,pistonValve:E,ports:D,drainCocks:O,crosshead:k,slideBars:A,mainRod:j,crankpin:M,couplingRods:N,drivingWheels:P,drivingAxles:F,leadingBogie:I,trailingTruck:L,returnCrank:R,eccentricRod:z,expansionLink:B,radiusRod:V,reverseShaft:H,combinationLever:U,unionLink:W,valveSpindle:G,smokebox:K,blastpipe:q,chimney:J,exhaustPassages:Y,blower:X,injector:Z,clackValve:Q,feedPipes:$,waterGauge:ee,tenderTank:te,airCompressor:ne,mainReservoir:re,brakeCylinder:ie,brakeShoes:ae,brakePipe:oe,brakeValve:se,cab:ce,regulatorHandle:le,reverser:ue,pressureGauge:de,frames:fe,springs:pe,bufferBeam:me,runningBoards:he,sandDome:ge,lubricator:_e,headlamps:ve,tender:ye,coalBunker:be,tenderBogies:xe,drawbar:Se,carriages:Ce};export{ne as airCompressor,n as ashpan,q as blastpipe,m as blowdown,X as blower,s as boilerBarrel,d as boilerWater,ie as brakeCylinder,oe as brakePipe,ae as brakeShoes,se as brakeValve,r as brickArch,me as bufferBeam,ce as cab,Ce as carriages,J as chimney,Q as clackValve,be as coalBunker,U as combinationLever,N as couplingRods,M as crankpin,k as crosshead,S as cylinder,we as default,O as drainCocks,Se as drawbar,F as drivingAxles,P as drivingWheels,_ as dryPipe,z as eccentricRod,Y as exhaustPassages,B as expansionLink,$ as feedPipes,c as fireTubes,e as firebox,i as fireholeDoor,l as flues,fe as frames,o as fusiblePlug,t as grate,ve as headlamps,Z as injector,I as leadingBogie,_e as lubricator,re as mainReservoir,j as mainRod,C as piston,w as pistonRod,E as pistonValve,D as ports,de as pressureGauge,V as radiusRod,le as regulatorHandle,g as regulatorValve,R as returnCrank,H as reverseShaft,ue as reverser,he as runningBoards,p as safetyValves,ge as sandDome,A as slideBars,K as smokebox,pe as springs,a as stays,T as steamChest,h as steamDome,b as steamPipes,f as steamSpace,y as superheaterElements,v as superheaterHeader,ye as tender,xe as tenderBogies,te as tenderTank,L as trailingTruck,u as tubePlates,W as unionLink,G as valveSpindle,ee as waterGauge,x as whistle};